Diagnosis:
The failing test `testPrimitiveTypeClassSerialization()` attempts to clone primitive type class objects (e.g., int.class, boolean.class) using `SerializationUtils.clone(Class<?>)`. The cloning method internally serializes and deserializes the object. The error reported is `ClassNotFoundException` during deserialization when cloning these primitive class types.

The cloning process in `SerializationUtils.clone(T object)` serializes the object to a byte array and then deserializes it using `ClassLoaderAwareObjectInputStream`. The deserialization uses its own `resolveClass(ObjectStreamClass desc)` method to load classes by name using a given class loader and a fallback to the thread context class loader.

The issue arises because primitive types like `int.class` do not correspond to a typical class name that can be resolved through `Class.forName()`. The standard `Class.forName()` throws `ClassNotFoundException` when asked to load primitive types by name (e.g., "int", "boolean"). This failure happens in `resolveClass` method in `ClassLoaderAwareObjectInputStream`:
```java
protected Class<?> resolveClass(ObjectStreamClass desc) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
     String name = desc.getName();
     try {
         return Class.forName(name, false, classLoader);
     } catch (ClassNotFoundException ex) {
         return Class.forName(name, false, Thread.currentThread().getContextClassLoader());
     }
 }
```
Here, `desc.getName()` for primitive classes yields names like "int", "boolean", etc., which `Class.forName()` cannot resolve. Therefore, cloning fails with `ClassNotFoundException`. Primitive classes are a special case in Java serialization and are not resolved by `Class.forName()`; they need to be handled explicitly.

Hence, the cause of the bug is missing special-case handling for primitive class names in the deserialization class resolution step inside `ClassLoaderAwareObjectInputStream.resolveClass`. The method blindly tries `Class.forName()` for all class names, which breaks when the class name corresponds to a primitive type.

No relevant hints were present in recent bug reports; this problem is quite specific to Java serialization and class loading mechanics with primitive class literals.

The patch should add special-case support for primitive class names in `resolveClass` to map them to the corresponding primitive Class objects (e.g., "int" → int.class) instead of calling `Class.forName()` on their names. This will prevent the `ClassNotFoundException` when cloning primitive class objects.

